   GET   /employers/{employerId}/members      listEmployerMembers      200 400 401 404
   GET   /members/{memberId}                  getMember                200 400 401 404
   GET   /members/{memberId}/contributions    listMemberContributions  200 400 401 404  (deprecated)
   GET   /contributions                       listContributions        200 400 401 404
   POST  /members/{memberId}/change-requests  createChangeRequest      201 400 401 404 409
   GET   /change-requests/{changeRequestId}   getChangeRequest         200 400 401 404

## 2. The client is generated, and generated code is checked in sync
   concept: openapi-typescript turns the spec into path, parameter and schema types; openapi-fetch is a typed fetch over them. CI regenerates and compares, so the committed client cannot lag behind the spec
   $ npx openapi-typescript openapi/v2.yaml -o .tmp/api.d.ts
   | 🚀 openapi/v2.yaml → .tmp/api.d.ts [165.6ms]
   -> exit 0
   src/generated/api.d.ts: 403 lines, identical to a fresh generation

## 3. Requests are validated against the spec at runtime
   concept: before any handler runs, path, query and body are checked against the operation's schemas; the 400 is an RFC 9457 problem that lists every mismatch, and unknown query parameters are refused rather than ignored
   provider (handlers fixed, response validation: enforce) on http://localhost:53043
   GET /contributions?memberId=M0001&limit=99&year=2026 -> 400 [{"where":"query.year","message":"is not a parameter of this operation"},{"where":"query.limit","message":"must be <= 24"}]
   POST /members/M0002/change-requests {kind: phone, value: "", effectiveDate: 2026-02-30, urgent: true} -> 400
     body: must NOT have additional properties (urgent)
     body/kind: must be equal to one of the allowed values
     body/value: must NOT have fewer than 1 characters
     body/effectiveDate: must match format "date"
   GET /members/M0001 without a token -> 401, WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm="members"

## 4. A consumer uses the typed client
   concept: an employer system calls the API through openapi-fetch; a middleware reads the Deprecation, Sunset and Link headers and reports each deprecated call once, so the consumer learns about the sunset from the API itself
   active Acme members: M0001 Alice Martin, M0002 Bruno Petit
   M0001 years of service on 2026-10-03: 12
   [consumer] DEPRECATED GET /members/{memberId}/contributions since 2026-10-01, sunset Wed, 31 Mar 2027 23:59:59 GMT, use /contributions?memberId=M0001
   M0001 total paid: 3909.00 through the deprecated endpoint, 3909.00 through GET /contributions in 3 pages of 5
   deprecation: @1790812800
   sunset: Wed, 31 Mar 2027 23:59:59 GMT
   link: </contributions?memberId=M0001>; rel="successor-version", </docs#tag/contributions/operation/listMemberContributions>; rel="deprecation"; type="text/html"
   change request: 201 CR0001 at /change-requests/CR0001; again: 409 CR0001 is already pending for this member and kind

## 5. Drift: a handler sends what the spec does not say
